tts F3 "Lately I have been keeping a single notebook for everything, instead of juggling many different places for my notes. I write down meetings, ideas, and shopping lists on the same pages, and I review the whole notebook every Sunday evening. That weekly review is where the magic happens, because I can see what I planned and what I actually finished. Anything that did not get done gets a decision, either I move it to next week or I drop it for good. My notebook is nothing fancy, just plain paper and a pen, but it works better for me than any digital tool I have tried."
tts F4 "I have also changed the way I consume long form content. Instead of letting my feed choose what I read, I keep a small list of topics I genuinely care about, and I search for those specific things when I have time. When I find a long article or an interview, I read it in two or three short sessions rather than trying to finish it in one sitting. That way I actually remember what I learned, and I can take notes while the ideas are still fresh in my head. It feels slower at first, but the retention is so much better that I never went back to my old habits."
tts F5 "Email used to be the thing that ate my whole day, so I made a rule about it. I only open my inbox twice a day, once in the morning and once in the late afternoon, and I keep my reply folder very small. Everything else stays unread until one of those two windows. At first I was worried I would miss something urgent, but after a few weeks I realized that almost nothing actually needs an answer within the hour. People wait for my reply just fine, and I get to spend the middle of my day on the work that actually moves my projects forward."
tts F6 "Every Friday I do a quick review of the week that just ended. I look at my calendar and my task list, and I ask myself three questions. What went well, what went wrong, and what will I do differently next week. I write the answers on one page and I keep that page visible all weekend. The routine sounds simple, but it changed the way I plan, because I stopped repeating the same mistakes week after week. When Monday comes, I already know exactly what needs my attention, and I do not waste the first hour of the week trying to figure out where to start."
tts F7 "I used to believe that the best way to get more done was to work longer hours, but I have completely changed my mind. I now pay attention to my energy instead of my clock, and I schedule my hardest thinking for the hours when I feel sharpest. For me that is the late morning, so I protect that time and leave meetings for the afternoon. I also stopped skipping lunch, which sounds obvious, but I used to eat at my desk while answering messages. Now I step away for a real break, and I come back with a clear head and better ideas."
