tts F9 "Most of my projects fail in the planning phase, not because the ideas are bad, but because the next step is unclear. So I started writing down the very next action for every project, and I keep that list at the top of my task manager. When I finish one action, the list tells me the next one, and the project keeps moving without me having to think about it. This sounds like a tiny change, but it removed most of the friction from my work. I no longer stare at a big scary project and wonder where to start, because the next step is always written down and waiting for me."
tts F10 "I have also learned to take breaks seriously. For years I believed that pushing through exhaustion was a sign of discipline, and I was wrong about that. Now I take a short break every ninety minutes, and I use that time to stretch, drink water, and look out the window at something far away. My eyes feel better, my back feels better, and I make fewer mistakes in the afternoon. The funny thing is that I get the same amount of work done in fewer hours, because my focus is stronger when I actually sit down to work."
